4. Class & Object (Foundation)
Class:
A blueprint that defines the object properties and methods.

class Car {
    String model;
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving");
    }
}

Object:
Any entity that has state and behavior is known as an object. A runtime instance of a class.

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c = new Car();
        c.drive();
    }
}

Output
Car is driving

5. Encapsulation (Data Hiding)
Definition
Encapsulation binds data and methods together and restricts direct access using access modifiers.

Interview one-liner
“Encapsulation hides internal state and exposes controlled access.”

Example
class Person {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age > 0) this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person p = new Person();
        p.setAge(25);
        System.out.println(p.getAge());
    }
}

Output
25

✔ Achieved using private + getters/setters
✔ Improves security and maintainability

6. Inheritance (IS-A Relationship)
Definition
Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and behavior of another class.

Interview one-liner
“Inheritance promotes code reusability using IS-A relationship.”

Example
class Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle started");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c = new Car();
        c.start();
    }
}


Output
Vehicle started

Types of Inheritance in Java

Single ✔
Multilevel ✔
Hierarchical ✔
Multiple ❌ (via class)
Multiple ✔ (via interface)

7. Polymorphism (Many Forms)
Definition
Polymorphism allows a method to behave differently based on object type.

Interview one-liner
“Polymorphism allows one interface, multiple implementations.”

7.1 Compile-Time Polymorphism (Method Overloading)
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator c = new Calculator();
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
        System.out.println(c.add(10.5, 20.5));
    }
}

Output
30
31.0

✔ Same method name
✔ Different parameters

7.2 Runtime Polymorphism (Method Overriding)
class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();
    }
}

Output
Child


✔ Achieved via inheritance
✔ Decided at runtime

8. Abstraction (Hiding Implementation)
Definition
Abstraction shows what to do, not how to do.

Interview one-liner
“Abstraction exposes behavior while hiding implementation.”

Using Abstract Class
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}

class Bike extends Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Bike started");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Bike();
        v.start();
    }
}


Output
Bike started

Using Interface:
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}

class CardPayment implements Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Payment via Card");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Payment p = new CardPayment();
        p.pay();
    }
}


Output
Payment via Card

9. Interface vs Abstract Class (🔥 Interview Table)
| Feature              | Interface           | Abstract Class      |
| -------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Methods              | Abstract + default  | Abstract + concrete |
| Multiple inheritance | ✔                   | ❌                   |
| Variables            | public static final | Any                 |
| Constructor          | ❌                   | ✔                   |


